REPRODUCER (probed vs perl 5.40.3):

    sub h { my @d = @_; scalar @d }
    my @w; for my $i (1 .. 2) { my $c = my $d = 0; push @w, sub { ++$d }; $w[-1]->() for 1 .. $i }
    print "10 ", join(",", map { $_->() } @w), "\n";
    my @x; for my $i (1 .. 2) { foo(my $q = 0); push @x, sub { ++$q }; $x[-1]->() for 1 .. $i }
    print "12 ", join(",", map { $_->() } @x), "\n";
    sub foo { $_[0] } sub k { my @q; }

perl: `10 2,3` / `12 2,3`.   PCL: `10 3,4` / `12 3,4` -- one shared cell for the loop's `my $d` / `my $q`.
Rename `@d` / `@q` in the named subs and PCL answers 2,3.

WHY: Pl/Parser2.pm `_embedded_my_veto_names` (the FILE-level veto of an expression-embedded `my`, #199) text-matches the BARE name under ANY sigil (`[\$\@\%]|\$\#`) in every named sub of the segment, and its exemption (`_sub_declares_name` + `!_sub_freely_references_name`) checks the EXACT name -- so a sub that declares and uses its own `@d` vetoes `$d` (the comment says so: "a sub that mentions a different sigil of the same base ($fh vs @fh) still vetoes").  The vetoed `my` takes the forward-defvar'd global, ONE cell per program.
FIX DIRECTION: the veto's question is a BINDER fact -- "does a named sub OTHER than the one holding the statement use THIS binding" (`Pl::Binder::uses_of` of the declarator's binding, then: is a use inside such a sub) -- ask it instead of the text scan (standing rule: no new scope walk).  Bar: the reproducer = perl; corpus-diff + the wide A/B; the gate-SET rule (a refusal/veto narrows).


