FIXED (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-08, generation v2-12900; commit 32ce58bd).  `_embedded_my_veto_names` asks the BINDER: for each of the statement's embedded declarators, the binding it makes (`decl_binding`), its uses (`uses_of`) -- and, where a string eval sees it, the literal-text uses in the sub's range (`text_uses_in`, port 2's rule) -- and vetoes only when one lies inside a named sub OTHER than the statement's own.  The sigil-blind text scan and its exemption walks `_sub_declares_name`, `_sub_freely_references_name`, `_decl_scope_block` are DELETED (a sub's own `my $fh` is another binding; an inner shadow's use is the shadow's).  Probed (scratch/s536e/pr/m5*.pl): the reproducer's 10 / 12 lines, `%d` and `&d` spellings, Capture-Tiny's open-then-sub shape (#199: still shares), a sub defined BEFORE the statement (perl reads the global: was a silent wrong, the veto shared the lexical's cell), a sub reading the handle + an inner `my $fh`, a sub's eval('$z'), a BEGIN/END block -- tree = perl.  Not this: a named sub INSIDE the loop body capturing the embedded `my` (perl binds the first iteration's) -> #4663, pre-existing.  Guard Pl/t/while-my-closure-01.t row 9 (fails on the base).  Emission: perl-tests/my.t + t/op/my.t (`{ @x = my @y }`), t/porting/customized.t, core Term/Cap.pm (`chomp(my @entry = <DATA>)`) -- now fresh lets.
